    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Build succeeds, all tests green. The audit log confirms fiscal-year behavior: a `2026-09-04` registration (September, month \u2265 7) now yields `M27` (FY2027), where the old calendar-year logic would have produced `M26`.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved date-derived values to a fiscal-year basis (FY starts 1 July). I introduced one small cohesive helper and routed the three date-derived values through it, rather than growing any existing method:\n\n- **`FiscalYear.java`** (new, `mapper` package) \u2014 the only place the July-1 rule lives: `of(date)` gives the four-digit fiscal year (July\u2013December roll into the next calendar year), `label(date)` formats `FY<YY>`.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping` wiring `fiscalYear` to `FiscalYear.label(registrationDate)`.\n- **`MembershipNumber`** \u2014 its year segment now uses `FiscalYear.of(...)` instead of `registrationDate.getYear()`.\n- **`MembershipPoints`** \u2014 tenure now counts elapsed fiscal years (`FiscalYear.of(now) - FiscalYear.of(reg) > 1`) instead of a 365-day window; dropped the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared the read-only `fiscalYear` property (so the generated DTO gets the field) and refreshed the `membershipNumber` description.\n\nThe registration date is already business-day-adjusted when stored (`BuildOwner` calls `BusinessDay.onOrAfter`), so all three derivations read from that adjusted date automatically \u2014 `SaveOwner`'s audit line picked up the fiscal-year membership number with no change. Each edited function stays at CC 1 in a small class, and the shared rule avoids duplicating the fiscal-year logic across the three call sites.",
